sSeed, while other albums with 1 item show correctly. If the grid deduplicated by album name, it'd be one card. Why would only IrisSeed split? Because the two files are in different volumes. The restore put huge on external_primary. That's the app's restore bug, not cache.\n\nCould the developer consider restoring to a different volume acceptable? They might have auto-rejected. Hmm.\n\nLet's check the actual restore behavior for other items: Zulu hughuge restored earlier also got external_primary/file/37. Yet it's in a single Zulu album card, not duplicated, because there were no other Zulu files. If it had another Zulu file on \"external\", it would split too. And Beta big(1) restored got external_primary too; Beta has only that item, so no split. So the restore *always* restores to a different volume. The split appears only when the album had another item on the original volume. That's why the developer's simple test maybe didn't include another item. But IrisSeed precondition exactly has 2, and trashing one then restoring causes split. That seems like an intended test case! This could be the intended album browsing bug.\n\nMaybe the developer rejected because my steps didn't mention the album also had another item? I did. Hmm. Or because they expected the app to group by bucket+volume and that's correct? But the precondition says IrisSeed has 2 photos. The QA expectation: one album.\n\nLet me reproduce once more from the current state to strengthen: trash big.jpg from one IrisSeed (right card), then restore it, see if now there are two or three IrisSeed cards. If each restored item goes to external_primary and original big is external, we'd still have 2 cards (one external_primary containing huge+big, one external containing big). Wait currently big is in the orange card (external volume) and huge is external_primary card. If I trash big and restore it, it'll go to external_primary, merging with huge into one card? That would test. But risky. Alternatively, the duplicate is stable.\n\nActually, maybe the correct interpretation: The app treats \"external\" and \"external_primary\" as separate storage volumes, and albums from each volume are separate but shown with same name. The bug is that the UI doesn't distinguish them, not grouping. But still.\n\nLet me not overdo this. The developer rejected; move on.\n\nLet's investigate the thumbnail cache bug directly. Look at the album grid: Does the cover thumbnail correspond to the correct photo? We know each photo's color: big=orange, huge=blue. For IrisSeed blue card contains huge (blue) correct; orange card contains big (orange) correct. For Beta orange contains big(1) (orange) correct. For Zulu blue contains hughuge (blue) correct. For apple orange big (orange) correct. So covers match.\n\nWhat might the new thumbnail cache break? Perhaps album cover thumbnails don't refresh after **replacing** (not deleting) the cover photo, or after **renaming**. Rename is available in details. If we rename a cover photo, its thumbnail may still show old image (fine) but the album card's content description updates. Hmm.\n\nMaybe the issue is rotation: album grid headers might reload and cover thumbnails become blank/wrong. In landscape screenshot, covers rendered fine. After rotating, covers still fine.\n\nMaybe the bug is caching across Media and Albums: if you open a photo in media (large) then the album cover could show the wrong image? Unlikely.\n\nCould be the new cache leaks the *wrong album's* cover when two albums share the same first photo filename. We have IrisSeed/big.jpg and apple/big.jpg. If the cache key is filename (not path), the apple cover might show IrisSeed's big thumbnail (same image anyway). Both orange, can't detect. But the content description of apple says \"big.jpg\" fine.\n\nPerhaps the cache bug is about the **header** on the album detail screen? There is no cover header in album detail. Hmm, maybe the \"album grid header\" refers to the top bar in album grid? No